# AIForge environment — copy to `.env` and edit:  cp .env.example .env
# `./run.sh` sources this automatically. Lines are KEY=value (no export/quotes).
# NEVER commit a real `.env` — it's gitignored.
#
# SINGLE MODE: everything runs on the host with embedded SQLite + scoped-OKR
# Markdown memory + Aider RepoMap + CodeGraph. No Postgres/Neo4j/Docker.
# The ONLY thing you MUST set is the model endpoint below.

# ──────────────────────────── Model endpoint (REQUIRED) ──────────────
# OpenAI-compatible base_url for your model. LM Studio / vLLM / Ollama /
# llama.cpp / TGI / LiteLLM all work. Use https:// for a TLS-fronted box.
AIFORGE_LM_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:1234/v1
# API key for the endpoint (blank for keyless local servers).
# AIFORGE_LM_API_KEY=

# Per-role model endpoints (optional — one served model per role):
#   AIFORGE_DOER_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:1234/v1
#   AIFORGE_PLANNER_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:1235/v1
#   AIFORGE_OPENAI_COMPAT_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:1234/v1   # fallback for all roles
#   AIFORGE_OPENAI_COMPAT_API_KEY=

# ─────────────────────────────── TLS / SSL ───────────────────────────
# Applies only to AIForge's own self-hosted services (model endpoint, MCP,
# local probes). Public hosts (GitHub, web fetches) are ALWAYS verified.
# Keep verify ON and trust an internal CA:
#   AIFORGE_LLM_CA_BUNDLE=/path/to/internal-ca.pem
# OR relax it for an internal/LAN box you control (default: true):
AIFORGE_LLM_SSL_VERIFY=true

# ───────────────────────────── CodeGraph (optional) ──────────────────
# Comma-separated repo paths to index for the enforced codegraph_* tools.
# The binary auto-installs (npm @colbymchenry/codegraph). Empty = skip.
# AIFORGE_CODEGRAPH_REPOS=/path/repoA,/path/repoB
# AIFORGE_CODEGRAPH_BIN=/custom/path/to/codegraph   # if not on PATH

# ──────────────────────────── Tracing (optional) ─────────────────────
# Self-hosted Langfuse trace UI — the ONE optional Docker piece. Enable with
# `./run.sh --with-langfuse` or:
# AIFORGE_LANGFUSE=1

# ─────────────────────────────── Misc (optional) ─────────────────────
# Clamp the chat agent's filesystem scope (default: UNRESTRICTED host access):
# AIFORGE_WORKSPACE_DIR=/path/to/allowed/workspace
# Listen host/port (defaults 127.0.0.1:8799):
# AIFORGE_HOST=127.0.0.1
# AIFORGE_PORT=8799

# ─────────────────────── External Postgres (rare, opt-in) ────────────
# The app is SQLite-only by default. To use a real external Postgres INSTEAD,
# set BOTH (a stale AIFORGE_PG_URL alone is ignored):
# AIFORGE_KEEP_PG=1
# AIFORGE_PG_URL=postgresql://user:pass@host:5432/aiforge

# ── Shared memory: hub and spoke ──────────────────────────────────────
# Every machine compacts its own memory locally. ONE machine additionally runs
# the cross-machine merge: start that box with `./run.sh --admin` (which writes
# AIFORGE_ROLE=admin into this file, so a restart cannot silently demote it —
# a demoted admin retires its merged fold). `./run.sh --spoke` hands the role
# back, which is how you MOVE the admin to another box.
#
# On every OTHER machine, name the admin here and leave AIFORGE_ROLE unset:
# AIFORGE_ADMIN_URL=http://rig.lan:8799
#
# The admin's peer id is learned from its first reply and cached in
# ~/.aiforge/admin.json. Pin it only if you would rather not trust that reply:
# AIFORGE_ADMIN_ID=rig
#
# The sync surface (/api/memory/sync/*) answers WITHOUT a credential by
# default, so a spoke needs no secret. That is only safe on a trusted
# interface — a LAN or a WireGuard address. To require the API token on it
# too, set this on the admin AND set the same AIFORGE_API_TOKEN on every spoke:
# AIFORGE_SYNC_AUTH=1

# ── Groups: one admin, several independent fleets ─────────────────────
# Leave all of this unset and the admin serves ONE pool of knowledge, exactly
# as it always has. Set it on the ADMIN to serve several — different customers,
# different sites — each with its own tree that no other group can read:
# AIFORGE_SYNC_GROUPS=cellular,retail
#
# Groups can also be created at runtime (POST /api/admin/groups). Once the file
# exists it is the record, and this line is ignored — so an operator who has
# added a group through the API is not overwritten by a stale line here.
#
# A CLIENT discovers the list from the admin url it already has. One group is
# joined automatically; several means it asks, in Settings -> Memory sync, and
# sends NOTHING until one is picked (knowledge in the wrong pool cannot be
# taken back). Pin it instead on a headless box that will never see that screen:
# AIFORGE_SYNC_GROUP=cellular
#
# Before every fold and every pull that changes something, the received tree is
# snapshotted by hardlink so it can be rolled back
# (GET/POST /api/admin/memory/snapshots|revert). How many to keep:
# AIFORGE_SYNC_SNAPSHOTS=10

# ── What a machine will NOT send ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every client filters its own notes before advertising them, so credentials
# and idle searches ("what is the capital of France") never leave the machine
# that learned them. A note carrying a credential is held back WHOLE rather
# than edited, because the sentence around a secret usually identifies the
# system too. Settings -> Memory sync shows what was held back and why.
#
# The two tunable thresholds, both applied only to notes carrying no sign of
# the codebase — no file path, no identifier, no error string. Raise them if
# chatter is getting through; lower them if real notes are being held back:
# AIFORGE_FILTER_MIN_SUBSTANCE=80
# AIFORGE_FILTER_MIN_WORDS=8

# ── Predicting the next step ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# At the end of a chat turn one capped call works out the action you most
# likely want next, and either DOES it or OFFERS it as a chip under the reply.
# Acting needs two things, not one: the model must be confident AND the action
# must be safe and reversible. Reads and safe shell commands act; a file write
# or a commit acts only on a clean git tree, so "undo" still means something;
# anything that leaves the machine, spends money or cannot be undone is ALWAYS
# only offered, at any confidence.
#
# Turn it off entirely — the turn then ends exactly as it did before, and no
# call is made at all:
# AIFORGE_PREDICT_DISABLE=1
#
# Keep the suggestions but never let them act on their own ("always ask me"):
# AIFORGE_PREDICT_ACT=0
#
# Below this confidence nothing is shown at all, not even an offer: a guess the
# model itself doubts is noise, and noise teaches you to ignore the chip that
# matters.
# AIFORGE_PREDICT_MIN_CONFIDENCE=0.75
#
# The cap on that one call, and which model role makes it. It runs on a cheap
# role so the agent's own model is not tied up producing it.
# AIFORGE_PREDICT_TIMEOUT_S=10
# AIFORGE_PREDICT_ROLE=enhancer
#
# What you ACCEPT is remembered per repo and shown to the next prediction —
# that is the whole learning mechanism. Dismissals are recorded too, because a
# feature that learns only from its wins drifts. Credentials never reach that
# store: every row passes the same filter the memory sync uses, and a row it
# refuses is dropped rather than stored scrubbed.

# ── Outbound identity ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Model calls identify themselves as `aiforge/<version> (<your-login-name>)`, so
# a gateway operator can tell whose traffic is whose and which build sent it.
# Set this to override the whole string (a proxy/WAF that demands a specific
# audit value), or to `off` to send only `aiforge/<version>` with no username.
# AIFORGE_LLM_USER_AGENT=off

# ── Seeing the UI (ui_check / ui_ask) ────────────────────────────────────────
# A coding model is text-only: it can drive a browser but it cannot look at the
# result. `ui_check` closes that gap by screenshotting the running app and
# having a VISION model describe it — so point the `vision` role at a VLM.
# Without this the tool still captures the page and still reports console and
# network errors, but it says plainly that nothing could read the image.
#
# Example, LM Studio serving a VLM alongside the coder:
# AIFORGE_VISION_MODEL=qwen/qwen3.8-27b
# AIFORGE_VISION_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:1234/v1
#
# Which role is consulted when the chat model itself can't see. Capability is
# detected from the endpoint (registry flag, then a live image probe), never
# from the model's name — a local VLM is called whatever its operator named it.
# AIFORGE_VISION_ROLE=vision
#
# Token budget for one audit. Deliberately generous: a THINKING VLM spends this
# allowance on reasoning first, and a small cap returns an empty answer with
# the whole budget burnt before a single visible word. When that happens the
# tool says so and names this knob — it never passes the reasoning trace off
# as the audit.
# AIFORGE_UI_AUDIT_MAX_TOKENS=1200

# ── Integration TLS (Jira / Confluence / GitLab) ─────────────────────────────
# Verification is OFF unless you turn it on: an unset {PREFIX}_INSECURE_TLS
# skips it, because these integrations point at internal, often self-signed,
# endpoints. That means the API TOKEN travels over a connection whose
# certificate nobody checked — worth knowing, and worth fixing per-endpoint.
#
# The better answer for a self-signed internal endpoint is to trust its CA —
# verification stays ON — so reach for this first:
# JIRA_CA_BUNDLE=/etc/ssl/certs/internal-ca.pem
# AIFORGE_CA_BUNDLE=/etc/ssl/certs/internal-ca.pem   (applies to all three)
#
# Force verification on for one integration (no CA bundle needed if the cert
# chains to a public root):
# JIRA_INSECURE_TLS=0
# CONFLUENCE_INSECURE_TLS=0
# GITLAB_INSECURE_TLS=0

# ── Web access: what the agent can reach ─────────────────────────────────────
# There is NO web search on this install. It was removed on purpose (2026-09-03):
# the query string is outbound data, and whatever the model typed into it — a
# line from your logs, a symbol name, a customer name — left the box to a
# third-party search engine with no filter in front of it. Reading a page is a
# different bargain: the destination is one the user named, so it survived, and
# it is OFF by default anyway.
#
# Allow the agent to fetch a page it was pointed at (web_fetch / web_crawl).
# The CODE default is off — but run.sh exports it as 1 unless you set it, so on
# a normal install it is ON. To lock a box down you must set it explicitly here
# (or use the hard-off below); leaving this line commented does NOT give you
# "off". The SSRF guard still blocks loopback, private ranges and cloud
# metadata even when this is on:
# AIFORGE_ALLOW_WEB_FETCH=0
#
# Hard-off, regardless of the flag above. Set this on a box that must not talk
# to the open web at all. AIFORGE_WEB_SEARCH_DISABLE is still honoured under its
# old name, so a machine already locked down does not quietly reopen:
# AIFORGE_WEB_FETCH_DISABLE=1
#
# Per-request timeout in seconds (default 12, or 15 on the doer paths):
# AIFORGE_WEB_TIMEOUT_S=12
#
# What these switches do and do not buy you, stated plainly:
#  * Both are honoured by every outbound page path: web_fetch, web_crawl,
#    fetch_url, http_get, the researcher's web_read, ui_check's readiness
#    probe, the @-mention URL expander, the note link-checker, Confluence
#    inline-image upload, and `browse` against an external host. A dev server
#    on this machine or the LAN keeps working under the lockdown — that is not
#    egress. An operator BROWSER allowlist is treated as deliberate permission
#    for those hosts and survives AIFORGE_ALLOW_WEB_FETCH=0, but not the
#    hard-off.
#  * A search engine's result URL is refused on every one of those paths,
#    whatever the switches say — otherwise deleting the search tool would mean
#    nothing, the agent would just fetch the URL. Redirects: web_fetch checks
#    each hop BEFORE making it; the doer's fetch_url/web_read follow the hop
#    first and refuse the RESULT, so a redirect into a search engine there has
#    already put the query on the wire. crawl4ai's headless engine follows its
#    own redirects unguarded.
#  * With fetching ON, an agent can still put text in a URL it composes: a
#    path, a query string, even a hostname label (the DNS lookup leaves before
#    any refusal can apply). The refusal above stops the obvious engines; it is
#    not a boundary against deliberate exfiltration. The boundary is the switch.
#  * web_crawl may render a page with a headless browser (crawl4ai), which runs
#    the page's own JavaScript — that page can then make requests of its own.
#    On a box that must not talk out, turn fetching off rather than relying on
#    the fetch layer being GET-only.

# ── Outbound traffic to DECLARED destinations ────────────────────────────────
# The section above is about pages, where the model composes the URL. These
# switches are about hosts YOU configured: Jira/Confluence/GitLab, email,
# Langfuse, remote MCP servers. All are decided in aiforge_core/net/egress.py.
#
# Close every class at once — AND page fetching, which is the one destination
# the MODEL picks. It reads as "nothing leaves this box", so it now behaves that
# way; it used to close the four classes below and leave web_fetch / browse /
# crawl open, which is the wider door. Inference is unaffected: prompts still
# reach the model endpoint and the embed/rerank sidecars, because the agent
# cannot run without them.
# AIFORGE_EGRESS_OFF=1
#
# Or one class at a time:
# AIFORGE_INTEGRATION_DISABLE=1   Jira / Confluence / GitLab
# AIFORGE_EMAIL_DISABLE=1        SMTP send + IMAP read
# AIFORGE_TELEMETRY_DISABLE=1    Langfuse (it posts whole PROMPTS off-box)
# AIFORGE_MCP_DISABLE=1          remote MCP servers
#
# WHERE traffic may go. The allowlist is ALWAYS enforced and DEFAULTS TO DENY —
# it is not something you switch on. It works without being typed because most
# of it is DERIVED: the hosts of your configured integrations, the model
# endpoint, the embed/rerank sidecars, Langfuse, remote MCP endpoints and the
# memory-sync admin are allowed automatically, and stop being allowed when you
# unconfigure them. This machine and the LAN are never on the list and never
# need to be — they are not egress.
#
# Everything else is refused, INCLUDING ordinary web pages. Add the few extra
# hosts you want in Settings -> Egress (stored in $AIFORGE_CONFIG_DIR/
# egress.json), or here for a headless box that never opens the UI. Host-suffix
# matched, so "corp.example" covers jira.corp.example:
# AIFORGE_EGRESS_ALLOW_HOSTS=docs.python.org,pypi.org
#
# WRITES WITH NOBODY WATCHING. Interactive chat has an approver; the ticket
# pipeline and cron jobs do not, and the approval gate degrades to "allow" when
# no approver is attached — so an unattended run could file a ticket or send
# mail unreviewed. POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE to an integration, and any email, are
# now refused in that case. Set this to allow them again:
# AIFORGE_UNATTENDED_WRITES=1
#
# File uploads (Confluence attachments, inline images) are content leaving in
# bulk rather than a sentence, so they have their own switch on top:
# AIFORGE_UPLOAD_DISABLE=1
#
# DANGEROUS COMMANDS WITH NOBODY WATCHING. The same shape one level down, in
# EXECUTION rather than egress: the risk classifier's verdict turns into "ask",
# and an unattended run has no one to ask, so `curl | sh`, a secret exfil, mkfs
# or a fork bomb used to run anyway in the ticket pipeline. A DANGEROUS verdict
# is now a refusal when no approver is attached (caution-tier — sudo, a global
# install, a force-push — still runs, so autonomy is intact). For a trusted
# batch box that genuinely needs them:
# AIFORGE_UNATTENDED_DANGEROUS=1
#
# The same classifier now reads INSIDE a notebook cell: `!cmd`, `%%bash`,
# os.system(), subprocess(...) are lifted back out and assessed, because a cell
# is a shell with three extra characters and carried no command string for any
# gate to see. And with AIFORGE_SANDBOX_REQUIRED=1 the IPython kernel refuses
# outright: the Docker sandbox is wired into `bash` only, so a cell would
# otherwise be a silent host fallback — exactly what that setting forbids.
#
# THE REROUTE. A refusal you can walk around by changing transport is not a
# boundary: web_fetch refuses a host, the agent reruns it as `curl`, and when
# that is refused, inside a notebook cell with an HTTP library. All three are
# the same request. So a shell command that FETCHES (curl / wget / nc / httpie
# …) is now checked against this same policy and DENIED — not asked, because
# what is missing is an allowlist entry, not a human's blessing — and the
# notebook kernel gets the policy installed inside it, at getaddrinfo and at
# connect, before any cell runs.
#
# AND THE OTHER DIRECTION, which matters more: a command that sends OUR data
# out answers to the same rules — scp / rsync / ssh host 'cmd', an upload flag
# on curl (-d, -F, -T, -X POST), a mail client, a cloud CLI push (aws s3 cp,
# gsutil cp, docker push, npm publish), a git push to an explicit URL, and
# bash's own `> /dev/tcp/host/port`, which involves no binary at all. A WRITE
# additionally has to clear the two rules the integration path already had: the
# host must be writable (one added in Settings is readable only — nothing about
# adding a docs site says you may post our data there) and an unattended run
# may not write at all without AIFORGE_UNATTENDED_WRITES=1.
#
# Left alone on purpose: a LAN/loopback target (not egress), a cloud READ
# (`aws s3 ls`), and `git push origin main` — refusing a named remote on a
# normal working day is how a control gets switched off, and the caution tier
# already asks about a push.
# AIFORGE_KERNEL_EGRESS=0        do not install the in-kernel guard
#
# STILL NOT A SANDBOX, and the docs must not pretend otherwise: code in the
# kernel runs in the same process as the guard and can undo it, and a shell can
# open a socket without naming a fetcher. What the layers above stop is the
# ROUTINE reroute — which is what actually happens. If the box must not talk
# out, the boundary is an OS egress firewall or a network namespace, not this
# file.

# ── TLS: self-signed endpoints, without turning verification off ─────────────
# "Skip TLS verify" used to mean CERT_NONE with hostname checking off — scoped
# to one endpoint, deliberate, and still "anything on the path can be that host
# and we will never know". It now means TRUST THAT CERTIFICATE: the endpoint's
# cert is pinned on first use into $AIFORGE_CONFIG_DIR/security/trusted_certs/
# <host>.pem (0600) and every later connection verifies against it, hostname
# check included. A substituted or re-issued certificate fails and says so.
#
# AIFORGE_LLM_CA_BUNDLE=/path/ca.pem   better still: verify against your CA
# AIFORGE_TLS_NO_TOFU=1                do not pin on first use; a missing pin is
#                                      then an error, for a deployment that
#                                      distributes certificates itself
#
# Plain http to anything outside this machine/LAN is refused: the request, its
# query string and the page cross a network in the clear. A local dev server on
# http is unaffected.
# AIFORGE_ALLOW_CLEARTEXT_HTTP=1       an intranet service that genuinely has
#                                      no TLS

# ── Library upkeep: merging duplicate rules / skills / workflows ─────────────
# Every writer keys on a SLUG, so "run tests first" and "always run the tests"
# are two files saying one thing — the library grows by accretion and each
# duplicate is prompt overhead on turns that never use it. A nightly pass
# clusters near-duplicates (deterministically, no model), merges each cluster
# with ONE structured call at role=learner — which is what puts it under the
# background rate ceiling (compaction_rpm, 5/min by default) instead of
# competing with chat — and archives the originals under
# $AIFORGE_CONFIG_DIR/artifacts_archive/ before removing them.
#
# It never touches a bundled default (re-seeded on upgrade), a repo-local
# artifact (it belongs to that checkout) or a workflow carrying scripts, and it
# refuses a merge whose body lost content rather than trusting the model.
#
# AIFORGE_ARTIFACT_MERGE=0        turn the sweep off
# AIFORGE_MERGE_HOUR=4            local hour it runs
# AIFORGE_MERGE_SIMILARITY=0.72   how alike two artifacts must be to cluster
# AIFORGE_MERGE_MAX_PER_RUN=5     model calls per pass (the cost ceiling)
# AIFORGE_MERGE_MAX_CLUSTER=6     members per cluster
# AIFORGE_MERGE_MAX_CHARS=12000   a cluster bigger than this is skipped, never
#                                 truncated (a partial merge silently loses text)
# AIFORGE_MERGE_MIN_COVERAGE=0.6  how much of the inputs' wording the merged
#                                 body must still carry to be accepted
#
# On demand: GET /api/library/merge/preview (clusters as they stand, no model),
# POST /api/library/merge/run {"dry_run": true | "force": true | "limit": N},
# GET /api/library/merge/report (what the last pass did).

# ── Where credentials live, and who can read them ────────────────────────────
# ALL OF THEM, in ONE folder: $AIFORGE_CONFIG_DIR/security (dir 0700, files
# 0600). agent_config.json (per-role api_key), integrations.json (Jira /
# Confluence / GitLab / SMTP tokens), mcp_servers.json (MCP keys + headers) and
# runtime.env moved there — a legacy file in the config root is MOVED (never
# copied: a copy leaves the secret exactly where it was) the first time it is
# resolved, and on boot. One path to exclude from a backup, one to audit, and a
# directory mode that also covers whatever is created inside it later.
#
# AIFORGE_SECURITY_DIR=/mnt/vault/aiforge   put the folder elsewhere
# AIFORGE_SECURE_STORE=0                    rollback: keep every file in place
#
# NOT moved, deliberately: the SQLite databases (chat.db, memory.db, …). They
# are opened by path from several places, carry -wal/-shm siblings, and may be
# open when the boot repair runs — moving a live database to tidy a directory
# is a bad trade. They are still repaired to 0600.

# ── Next-step suggestions ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The chip at the end of a turn. Every prediction used to start from an empty
# room: the history recorded what was offered and what you said to it, and the
# prediction path read only the ACCEPTED rows. A dismissal fed back into
# nothing, so the same two or three suggestions arrived in every new chat.
#
# AIFORGE_PREDICT_REPEAT_H=24        a suggestion already made (in ANY chat, on
#                                    this repo) is not made again for this long
# AIFORGE_PREDICT_DISMISS_DAYS=14    one you DISMISSED stays gone this long, and
#                                    is shown to the model as do-not-repeat
# AIFORGE_PREDICT_TOOLLESS_MIN=0.9   a suggestion that cannot name the tool it
#                                    would use is advice, not an action, so it
#                                    clears a higher bar to be worth showing
# AIFORGE_PREDICT_MIN_CONFIDENCE=0.75  the ordinary floor
# AIFORGE_PREDICT_ACT=0              keep the suggestions, never auto-run one
# AIFORGE_PREDICT_DISABLE=1          no suggestions at all

# Under $AIFORGE_CONFIG_DIR (default ~/.aiforge):
#   agent_config.json   per-role LLM api_key
#   integrations.json   Jira / Confluence / GitLab / email tokens
#   runtime.env         UI-persisted env
#   chat.db memory.db   transcripts and notes — as revealing as the tokens
# New files are written 0600. Files that ALREADY existed keep whatever mode
# they had, so the API tightens the directory and these files on every boot and
# logs what it changed (that log is worth reading: it means they were readable
# by other local accounts until then, and a token they held may want rotating).
#
# Ask for group access instead of owner-only (directories get the matching
# execute bit):
# AIFORGE_CONFIG_MODE=0640
#
# Turn the boot-time repair off entirely:
# AIFORGE_SKIP_PERM_REPAIR=1
